1. Kd4 {The king, at his queen's fourth square.} Kd6 {The king, at his queen's third square. (b)
NOTE: Had he played his king to his king's bishop's fourth square, you would have again got the
move over him; which would have given you the game. The subject of a backgame,} 2. Kd3 {The
king, at his queen's third square.} Kd7 {The king, at his queen's second square. (c) NOTE: Had
he played his king to his queen's fourth square, he would have lost the game. A new subject of a
back-game.} 3. Ke3 {The king, at his third square.} Ke7 {The king, at his second square. (d)
NOTE: He keeps himself in such a situation, that he may oppose your king, when you please to
push him forwards, either to his fourth square, or to your queen's fourth square.} 4. Kd4 {The
king, at his queen's fourth square.} Kd6 {The king, at his queen's third square.} 5. Ke4 {The
king, at his fourth square.} Ke6 {The king, at his third square. (e) NOTE: Supposing that your
adversary makes his moves exactly, it is a drawn-game.} *
